WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.230
Aaleeyah Pringle Alim:
"You have to get your

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:02.638
wisdom teeth removed."

00:00:02.638 --> 00:00:04.924
Amin Elsaeed: OK, big myth here.

00:00:04.924 --> 00:00:06.780
Big, big, big myth.
[Pringle Alim laughs]

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.730
"Charcoal toothpaste is better
than regular toothpaste."

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.920
Pringle Alim: No. No, no, no.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.200
"There's no such thing
as brushing too much."

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.373
Elsaeed: Try to brush for
at least two minutes, right?

00:00:19.373 --> 00:00:21.210
Sing "Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star."

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.410
Pringle Alim: What? I
feel like that's so short.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:24.890
Elsaeed: No, like, sing the whole verse.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.260
Hey, everyone, my name
is Dr. Amin Elsaeed.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.410
I'm a dentist working out
of the Chicagoland area.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:33.800
And my name is Dr. Aaleeyah
Pringle Alim, also a dentist.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.526
And we are here to
debunk some dental myths.

00:00:36.526 --> 00:00:40.230
Elsaeed: Myth No. 1:
"Sugar causes cavities."

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.325
Pringle Alim: Oh, my gosh.

00:00:41.325 --> 00:00:44.820
Sugar actually is not
the cause of cavities.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.890
In your mouth, you have
active normal bacteria,

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:51.583
and when sugar is fed to that bacteria,

00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:54.224
the bacteria produces acid,

00:00:54.224 --> 00:00:56.650
and it creates cavities on teeth.

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:58.383
Elsaeed: So it's the fuel

00:00:58.383 --> 00:01:01.010
for the bacteria to cause the cavities.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:04.548
Pringle Alim: Myth two:
"Electric toothbrushes

00:01:04.548 --> 00:01:07.280
are better than regular toothbrushes."

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.610
Elsaeed: The most important thing

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.540
is that you are actually brushing, right?

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.160
So having that mechanical
movement against your teeth,

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.160
whether it be an electric toothbrush,

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.440
a regular toothbrush,

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.910
that's the most important
thing to prevent plaque

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.490
from sitting on the surfaces of our teeth.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.330
So if you have a regular toothbrush,

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:29.140
you can still do a good
job of brushing at home.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.190
Myth No. 3: "White teeth
are always healthy."

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.470
Pringle Alim: White teeth
really just have to do

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.320
with how your enamel
appears on the surface.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.280
So, your tooth actually has three layers.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.810
It has the inside core, which is the pulp.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.290
It has that middle layer,
which is yellow in color.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.740
And then it has enamel,
which shows up whiter.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.970
It's like the armor of your tooth.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.178
So, genetically speaking,

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.461
if you don't have the type of teeth

00:01:54.461 --> 00:01:57.090
that will appear whiter after whitening,

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.960
you'll have to go maybe veneers or crowns

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.320
to get a whiter, whiter, whiter smile.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.978
Elsaeed: It's very possible to have,

00:02:02.978 --> 00:02:05.677
you know, cavities, bone loss,

00:02:05.677 --> 00:02:08.260
periodontal disease, even
with pearly white teeth.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.240
Pringle Alim: All right, myth No. 4:

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.780
"There's no such thing
as brushing too much."

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.410
Elsaeed: Anytime you want to
take plaque off your teeth,

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.690
we're all for it.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:21.280
But then it comes to how
hard are you brushing, right?

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.720
Some people go in there,
and it's like a chain saw.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.237
They're just [makes chain-saw noise].

00:02:26.237 --> 00:02:27.890
And what ends up happening
is when you break down

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.380
that enamel by so much, so much
hard and vigorous brushing,

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.840
you end up getting sensitivity.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.673
So people are like,
"Oh, I drink something,

00:02:34.673 --> 00:02:35.730
and my teeth hurt."

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.710
Pringle Alim: Go and you
look at your toothbrush,

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.340
and if the bristles are
flared out like this,

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.370
you're doing it too hard.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.990
Elsaeed: Myth No. 5:
"Drinking with a straw

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:46.560
is better for your teeth."

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.040
Pringle Alim: This is kind of
true, but also kind of false.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.215
So, drinking through a straw

00:02:53.215 --> 00:02:55.881
is theoretically better for your teeth

00:02:55.881 --> 00:02:57.620
if done the right way.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.730
So, if you drink through the straw

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.690
and the liquid literally
just passes from the straw

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.720
maybe to your tongue to your throat

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.530
and you swallow, then that's OK.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.670
But the problem comes with

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:09.893
when people drink through the straw

00:03:09.893 --> 00:03:12.530
and they still kind of swish
it around in their mouths,

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.320
that kind of defeats the purpose

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.670
of drinking through the straw.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:16.808
It's damaging your teeth

00:03:16.808 --> 00:03:19.450
the longer that that
liquid sits on your teeth.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:22.040
So, drink it fast if you can,

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.990
drink it through a straw
the right way if you can,

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.400
and always, always, always
follow it up with water.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.360
If you can follow up your
acidic drinks with water,

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.350
you can help your body get
your pH back down to neutral

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.633
or back up to neutral quicker.

00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:39.500
Myth six:

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:43.160
"Brushing harder makes
your teeth cleaner."

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.185
Elsaeed: OK, brushing harder

00:03:45.185 --> 00:03:47.601
does not make your teeth cleaner.

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:51.245
What makes your teeth cleaner
is effective brushing.

00:03:51.245 --> 00:03:53.830
If you're brushing the right way, right?

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:55.755
If I take my toothbrush

00:03:55.755 --> 00:03:57.660
and I'm jamming it in there

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.560
with 600 pounds of force against my teeth,

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.530
I'm causing a lot of damage.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.500
So it's not about the strength;
it's more about the motion.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.290
Pringle Alim: Angle your
toothbrush a little bit

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.770
so that the bristles are
actually angled into the gums,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:11.930
and you move back and forth.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.620
Think about brushing two
to three teeth at a time,

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.670
instead of large, big strokes.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.980
Elsaeed: Myth No. 7. This is a good one.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:24.180
"Charcoal toothpaste is better
than regular toothpaste."

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.810
Pringle Alim: There is
no research that suggests

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.050
that charcoal toothpaste is better

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:32.320
than your normal fluoride
toothpaste. Zero.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.210
What people are kind of attracted to

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.148
about charcoal toothpaste
is that it promotes

00:04:38.148 --> 00:04:40.010
the idea that it will whiten your teeth.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.240
People went as far as
to taking raw charcoal,

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.150
like, just kind of charcoal powder,

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.740
and brushing their teeth with that

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.883
in hopes that they were going to, like,

00:04:48.883 --> 00:04:53.190
I don't know, unlock a
secret whitened-tooth door.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.320
No. No, no, no.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.270
So, what can end up happening is,

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.540
if you use charcoal too much,

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.880
you can end up wearing away your enamel,

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.150
which will then make your
teeth appear more yellow,

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:09.350
the opposite of what you want.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:14.350
Myth 8: "You have to get
your wisdom teeth removed."

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:17.013
Elsaeed: OK, big myth here.

00:05:17.013 --> 00:05:19.310
Big, big, big myth, I know.
[Pringle Alim laughs]

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:21.520
Listen, man, let me tell
you, let me tell you,

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.360
you do not have to get
your wisdom teeth removed.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:28.068
If there is a specific reason,

00:05:28.068 --> 00:05:29.918
like it's causing you pain,

00:05:29.918 --> 00:05:32.660
the tooth now has a giant cavity,

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.080
the tooth is impacted or now pushing up

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.110
against the tooth in front of it

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.590
and now it's just causing a whole drama

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:41.610
inside your mouth, right?

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:45.980
For a lot of people, that
can be a very valid reason

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:47.900
to have those teeth removed.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.690
And another reason that these teeth

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.330
should be removed sometimes is

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:55.360
getting to those third
molars by ourselves at home

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.530
and cleaning them, or these wisdom teeth,

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:59.210
can be very, very difficult.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.930
Pringle Alim: If they're
in line with the others

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.965
and if they're completely fine,

00:06:03.965 --> 00:06:06.200
yeah, you treat it like any other tooth.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:07.766
Elsaeed: Myth No. 9:

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:12.491
"You should brush right after every meal."

00:06:12.491 --> 00:06:15.789
Pringle Alim: After you eat,
your mouth becomes more acidic.

00:06:15.789 --> 00:06:18.230
So, it just naturally does. It
doesn't matter what you eat.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:21.010
And if you take something
mechanical, like bristles,

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:24.900
and rub it up against your
enamel in an acidic environment,

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.000
you can more easily wear down your enamel,

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:29.770
and you're doing more harm than good.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:32.210
So what you wanna do, you
wanna wait a little bit.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.090
You wanna wait at least 30 minutes

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:38.470
until your mouth gets
more basic, more neutral,

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:40.400
and then go in and brush.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.090
Elsaeed: I always tell
my patients, you know,

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:47.630
if you eat and then you have
food stuck in your teeth,

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:50.630
you're literally throwing a free buffet

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:52.750
to bacteria that didn't
pay to get in there.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:54.441
Pringle Alim: Myth 10:

00:06:54.441 --> 00:06:58.440
"Teeth grinding is not a serious problem."

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.181
Elsaeed: A lot of people

00:07:00.181 --> 00:07:02.400
don't know that they grind their teeth.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.945
A lot of it is you grind at night

00:07:04.945 --> 00:07:06.403
subconsciously when you're asleep.

00:07:06.403 --> 00:07:09.120
You don't even recognize
that you're doing it.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.320
So, what teeth grinding does, No. 1,

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:14.420
it can start wearing away at
the surfaces of your teeth.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.580
So, if you wear away the protective layer,

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.160
now you are at a much higher risk

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.230
of having way more sensitive teeth.

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:26.030
When you used to drink a
cold glass of water before

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:28.650
and didn't feel anything,
now there's a chance,

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.380
if you drink that cold glass
of water without enamel,

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:35.280
that you may have experiences
with some sensitivity.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.450
Another reason that grinding
is a serious problem

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:40.660
is that it can have effects on our jaw.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.370
Pringle Alim: If you're like
me and you're a grinder,

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:45.487
you definitely need to get a mouth guard.

00:07:45.487 --> 00:07:47.700
Teeth grinding is definitely on the rise.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:51.010
Elsaeed: Myth No. 11: "Gum bleeding means

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.670
you should stop flossing."

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:58.350
Pringle Alim: Do not stop
flossing because your gums bleed.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:07:59.890
You gotta just push through it.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.190
Gum bleeding basically means

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:06.760
that there has been so much
inflammation around your tooth

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:10.790
and so much potential foodstuff
that's kind of stuck there,

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:13.170
when you disturb it with the floss,

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:16.420
your gums get angry, and
then they start to bleed.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:18.760
Elsaeed: Flossing is never going to have

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:21.940
a negative effect on your oral cavity.

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:24.690
Pringle Alim: Coming in
hot with myth No. 12:

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:28.280
"Aligners will straighten your teeth."

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:31.860
Elsaeed: OK, this is a
very loaded question.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:33.940
What is the stability of their teeth?

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:36.450
How long are they going
to keep these teeth?

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:39.520
Are their gums healthy?
Is the bone healthy?

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:40.900
By jumping into aligners

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:44.120
without having that
very crucial knowledge,

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:45.760
there's a chance that when you're trying

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:47.540
to straighten these teeth,

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:49.430
you could lose these teeth.

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:52.620
Pringle Alim: A good
rule of thumb for me is,

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.850
if they don't take X-rays and
they wanna move your teeth,

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.940
you probably should not let them do that.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:00.832
Elsaeed: Myth lucky No. 13:

00:09:00.832 --> 00:09:04.210
"Toothaches always mean cavities."

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:07.030
Pringle Alim: When you
think about a cavity,

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:09.810
you think of a small hole in your tooth

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:11.650
that's gonna get filled with something.

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:13.500
You don't know what, the dentist does it,

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:15.070
and then it's fixed.

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:18.540
But a toothache can be
kind of an indicator

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.620
for something way more serious.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:23.995
So, it could possibly be an indication

00:09:23.995 --> 00:09:26.553
for bone loss.

00:09:26.553 --> 00:09:29.870
When you bite down and
you're having a lot of pain,

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.610
it could be because the
bone around your tooth

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:34.870
has started to resorb,
started to disappear.

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:38.268
It can be an indication of
a fracture in your tooth.

00:09:38.268 --> 00:09:41.610
Myth 14: "Chewing gum

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:43.920
is just as good as brushing."

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:46.703
Elsaeed: Chewing gum is nowhere near

00:09:46.703 --> 00:09:48.911
as good as brushing, right?

00:09:48.911 --> 00:09:51.560
So, chewing gum definitely has

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:55.330
some positive properties, OK?

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:57.840
No. 1, if you're gonna chew gum,

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.670
you wanna try to chew sugar-free gum.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:04.150
Or, if you wanna look for
gum that has xylitol in it,

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:07.730
which is a great sugar option in your gum,

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:08.870
what that's gonna do is

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:11.530
it's going to promote saliva production.

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:15.560
But, for as great as
chewing sugar-free gum is,

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:17.480
it will never take away

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:20.030
or be a substitute for brushing, right?

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.862
You have to go in there
with the mechanical work,

00:10:22.862 --> 00:10:25.886
toothbrush, whether electric, manual,

00:10:25.886 --> 00:10:28.569
and get those surfaces of those teeth

00:10:28.569 --> 00:10:30.161
clean and free of plaque.

00:10:30.161 --> 00:10:31.611
Pringle Alim: Your oral health

00:10:31.611 --> 00:10:34.836
is a part of your overall health.

00:10:34.836 --> 00:10:37.660
So, it's not something separate.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.130
Like, you can't just take your mouth out

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:40.240
and put it someplace.

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:42.440
You have to treat it like
it's a part of your body.

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:45.110
And so daily maintenance is crucial.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.743
Elsaeed: A lot of people have,
you know, dental anxiety.

00:10:47.743 --> 00:10:50.401
Like, it's normal. It's
a lot to think about.

00:10:50.401 --> 00:10:52.660
There are so many factors
that people think about

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:55.020
before deciding to go to a dentist.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.310
Sometimes going to the
dentist to just talk

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:00.340
can really make a difference.

00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:01.782
Pringle Alim: If you want more information

00:11:01.782 --> 00:11:04.519
about oral health and just best practices,

00:11:04.519 --> 00:11:06.397
you can always check out Ada.org,

00:11:06.397 --> 00:11:08.980
and they have tons of resources.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:10.780
Just type it into the search bar,

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:12.980
and something will pop
up that can help you.

